{title: Jesus, Still Lead On}
{artist: Nicolaus von Zinzendorf, tr. Jane Borthwick / Adam Drese}
{genre: Hymn}
{style: Traditional}
{flow: Meditative}
{key: G}
{tempo: 112}
{time: 4/4}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Cyber Hymnal score (Trinity Hymnal (Philadelphia, Pennsylvania: Orthodox Presbyterian Church, 1961), # 504)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]Jesus, [D]still [Em]lead [D]on, [Em]till [D]our [G]rest [D]be [G]won,
[G]And, [D]although the [G]way [D]be [G]cheer[D]less,
[Em]We [D]will [G]fol[D]low [Em]calm [B]and [Em]fear[B]less,
[Em]Guide [B]us [D]by [Em]Thy [D]hand [Em]to [D]our [G]fa[D]ther[G]land.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[G]If the [D]way [Em]be [D]drear, [Em]if [D]the [G]foe [D]be [G]near,
[G]Let [D]no faithless [G]fears [D]o’er[G]take [D]us,
[Em]Let [D]not [G]faith [D]and [Em]hope [B]for[Em]sake [B]us,
[Em]For [B]through [D]many [Em]a [D]woe [Em]to [D]our [G]home [D]we [G]go.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[G]When we [D]seek [Em]re[D]lief [Em]from [D]a [G]long [D]felt [G]grief;
[G]When [D]temptations [G]come [D]al[G]lur[D]ing,
[Em]Make [D]us [G]pa[D]tient [Em]and [B]en[Em]dur[B]ing;
[Em]Show [B]us [D]that [Em]bright [D]shore [Em]where [D]we [G]weep [D]no [G]more.
{end_of_verse}
